> Here is a draft adaptation-methods subsection that matches the style of your risk‑modelling text and the conventions you specified.
> 
> ---
> 
> \subsection{Modeling adaptation in heat-related mortality}
> 
> We incorporate adaptation into the heat-mortality damage function in two stages. First, we compile empirical estimates of the mortality benefits and costs of specific adaptation measures and use these to infer generic relationships between adaptation spending, climate conditions, and reductions in temperature-related vulnerability. Second, we embed these relationships into the temperature–mortality damage function, so that projected deaths respond endogenously to both global mean temperature and assumed adaptation investments.
> 
> The empirical calibration draws on four distinct adaptation channels. For building-level measures, we use estimates of the effectiveness of external shutters in reducing summertime temperature-related mortality in the UK under the UKCP09 medium-emissions scenario \citep{taylor2018projecting} (projecting impact of shutters on UK heat mortality). We combine these with estimates of capital costs per dwelling for internal shutters and current UK housing stock to obtain national-scale expenditures. For active cooling, we use evidence on the association between air-conditioning prevalence and heat-related mortality from Canada \citep{francesco2020aircon} (air-conditioning and heat mortality), together with UK-specific capital costs for portable and fixed units and projected electricity expenditures, to construct cost–benefit points at different penetration levels. For autonomous behavioural and technological adaptation, we use a global, empirically based model of temperature–mortality vulnerability that distinguishes total climate-related mortality from the component directly attributable to temperature, allowing the residual to be interpreted as adaptation-related mortality changes \citep{carleton2022mortality} (global temperature–mortality meta-analysis with income and adaptation). Finally, we use the estimated income elasticity of climate-related mortality from the same study to represent income-driven adaptation or improved health-system capacity.
> 
> For each adaptation measure $k$ (e.g.\ shutters, air conditioning, autonomous adaptation, income-related adaptation), we express the empirical evidence in terms of (i) an estimated proportional reduction in national vulnerability to heat-related mortality, defined as the ratio of projected excess deaths with and without the measure at a given level of global mean surface temperature change $\Delta T$, and (ii) an associated national annualised expenditure. Vulnerability reductions are constructed either directly from reported mortality changes (for shutters and air conditioning) or by decomposing total and temperature-attributable mortality estimates into an adaptation-related residual (for autonomous and income-related adaptation). For measures with reported uncertainty (e.g.\ air-conditioning effectiveness), we treat reported confidence bounds as defining a lognormal sampling distribution for the fractional change in vulnerability.
> 
> We convert these point estimates into a continuous vulnerability-response relationship. First, we relate proportional vulnerability reductions to global mean temperature by fitting a log-linear model of benefits as a function of $\Delta T$ using the shutter evidence; this provides a temperature-dependent scaling that captures the fact that a fixed measure yields larger absolute mortality benefits at higher temperatures. Second, we calibrate an aggregate cost–benefit relationship for adaptation by fitting a log–log model of the proportional reduction in vulnerability against total national adaptation expenditure, using the air-conditioning evidence as the primary calibration point. This yields a functional form in which the fractional reduction in vulnerability from adaptation spending $C$ satisfies
> \[
> \log B(C) = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \log C,
> \]
> with the implied benefits $B(C)$ approaching unity as $C$ grows large and diminishing marginal returns for increasing $C$. We propagate parameter uncertainty by drawing Monte Carlo samples $m$ from the estimated coefficient covariance matrices, generating an ensemble of benefit curves $B_m(C,\Delta T)$ that jointly reflect uncertainty in both temperature- and cost-responsiveness.
> 
> To link these calibration relationships to the baseline temperature–mortality damage function, we treat the no-adaptation excess heat deaths as a quadratic function of global mean temperature change,
> \[
> D^{\mathrm{base}}(\Delta T) = \alpha \Delta T + \beta \Delta T^2,
> \]
> where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are themselves estimated by meta-analysing several UK-focused projections of future heat-related mortality under alternative scenarios \citep{hajat2014health,jenkins2022heat,garcialeon2024heat,hsa2023heat,carleton2022mortality} (UK projections of future heat mortality under climate change). For each source, we interpret reported central projections and uncertainty ranges for excess deaths at different temperatures or time points as noisy observations of this quadratic form, and fit source-specific coefficients using repeated sampling from the implied normal distributions. Combining these across sources in a Monte Carlo ensemble yields a distribution of baseline mortality trajectories $D_m^{\mathrm{base}}(\Delta T)$ and associated uncertainty bands.
> 
> Adaptation is then represented as a multiplicative attenuation of these baseline damages. For a given temperature change $\Delta T$ and total adaptation expenditure $C$, the adapted excess deaths in Monte Carlo draw $m$ are given by
> \[
> D_m^{\mathrm{adapt}}(\Delta T, C) = D_m^{\mathrm{base}}(\Delta T)\,\bigl[1 - B_m(C,\Delta T)\bigr],
> \]
> where $B_m(C,\Delta T)$ is the fraction of baseline vulnerability removed by adaptation. For measures that primarily shift vulnerability independent of temperature (such as widespread deployment of active cooling), we treat $B_m$ as a function of $C$ alone and apply a temperature-independent scaling. For measures whose effectiveness is estimated at particular temperature levels (such as shutters), we apply both the cost–benefit scaling and the temperature elasticity so that the same level of investment produces greater absolute mortality reductions at higher $\Delta T$. For income-driven adaptation, we approximate future income effects by applying the estimated income elasticity of climate-related mortality to the projected change in per-capita income between a reference and future period, thereby obtaining an effective $B_m$ corresponding to income-driven vulnerability reductions without additional explicit adaptation spending.
> 
> We evaluate several stylised national adaptation policy scenarios, each defined by a total expenditure trajectory and a mix of measures. For each scenario, we apply the corresponding attenuation factor to the baseline meta-analytic mortality curve to obtain a set of temperature-dependent adapted mortality trajectories $\{D_m^{\mathrm{adapt}}(\Delta T, C)\}_m$. These are then transformed into monetary damages using the same value of a prevented fatality as in the baseline risk modelling, and expressed as annual losses relative to a UK GDP trajectory. The Monte Carlo ensemble provides central estimates and uncertainty ranges for adaptation-adjusted mortality damages under alternative levels of investment, which are then used in the broader meta-analytic synthesis across sectors described in \citet{rising2026comprehensive}.
