> \subsection{Modeling adaptation in heat-related mortality}
> 
> We represent adaptation as a reduction in temperature-related vulnerability that depends on both global mean temperature change and adaptation investment. We first compile empirical estimates of mortality benefits and costs for several adaptation channels: building-level shading (external shutters) in the UK under UKCP09 \citep{taylor2018projecting} (projecting impact of shutters on UK heat mortality), air conditioning and its association with heat-related deaths from Canadian evidence combined with UK-specific costs \citep{francesco2020aircon} (air-conditioning and heat mortality), and autonomous and income-driven adaptation inferred from the decomposition of total and temperature-attributable mortality and income elasticities in a global temperature–mortality study \citep{carleton2022mortality} (global temperature–mortality meta-analysis with income and adaptation). For each measure, we derive a proportional reduction in national vulnerability (the ratio of excess deaths with and without the measure) and an associated annualised national expenditure.
> 
> We use these points to estimate two generic relationships: a temperature dependence of vulnerability reduction, fitted as a log-linear function of global mean temperature change using the shutter evidence, and a diminishing-returns cost–benefit curve relating the proportional vulnerability reduction to total adaptation spending, fitted in log–log form using the air-conditioning evidence. Parameter uncertainty from these regressions is propagated by Monte Carlo sampling of coefficient vectors, generating an ensemble of benefit curves indexed by $m$. These are combined with the baseline, no-adaptation quadratic temperature–mortality damage function (estimated via meta-analysis of UK heat-mortality projections \citep{hajat2014health,jenkins2022heat,garcialeon2024heat,hsa2023heat,carleton2022mortality} (UK projections of future heat mortality under climate change)) to obtain adapted mortality as a multiplicative attenuation of baseline damages. For a given global mean temperature change $\Delta T$ and total adaptation expenditure $C$, excess heat deaths in draw $m$ are
