> ```latex
> \subsection{Modeling adaptation in heat-related mortality}
> 
> We represent adaptation as a temperature- and investment-dependent reduction in vulnerability to heat-related mortality. Empirical calibration draws on four channels: building-level shading via external shutters in the UK under UKCP09 \citep{taylor2018projecting} (projecting impact of shutters on UK heat mortality); air conditioning, using evidence on the association between air-conditioning prevalence and heat-related mortality from Canada combined with UK cost estimates \citep{francesco2020aircon} (air-conditioning and heat mortality); and autonomous and income-driven adaptation, inferred from the decomposition of total and temperature-attributable mortality and income elasticities in a global temperature–mortality study \citep{carleton2022mortality} (global temperature–mortality meta-analysis with income and adaptation). For each measure, we derive (i) a proportional change in national vulnerability, defined as the ratio of excess deaths with and without the measure at a given global mean surface temperature change $\Delta T$, and (ii) an associated annualised national expenditure.
> 
> We use these points to estimate two generic relationships. First, a temperature dependence of vulnerability reduction is fitted using the shutter evidence, relating proportional benefits to $\Delta T$ and capturing the increasing absolute impact of a fixed measure at higher temperatures. Second, we estimate a diminishing-returns cost–benefit curve linking proportional vulnerability reduction to total adaptation spending $C$, in log–log form using the air-conditioning evidence. Together, these yield a function $B(C,\Delta T)$ giving the fraction of baseline vulnerability removed by adaptation investment $C$ at temperature change $\Delta T$. Parameter uncertainty from both regressions is propagated by Monte Carlo sampling of the coefficient vectors, producing an ensemble of benefit curves $B_m(C,\Delta T)$ indexed by $m$. For income-driven adaptation, we apply the estimated income elasticity of climate-related mortality to projected changes in per-capita income between a reference and future period, which yields an implicit $B_m$ without explicit adaptation spending.
> 
> These calibrated responses are applied to the meta-analytic, no-adaptation temperature–mortality damage function. Baseline excess heat deaths are represented as a quadratic in global mean temperature change,
> \[
> D_m^{\mathrm{base}}(\Delta T) = \alpha_m \Delta T + \beta_m \Delta T^2,
> \]
> with coefficients $\alpha_m$ and $\beta_m$ obtained from Monte Carlo meta-analysis of UK-focused projections of future heat-related mortality \citep{hajat2014health,jenkins2022heat,garcialeon2024heat,hsa2023heat,carleton2022mortality} (UK projections of future heat mortality under climate change). For each scenario and year $t$, we take projected global mean temperature change $\Delta T_t$ and construct an effective cumulative adaptation stock from a constant annual investment $C^{\mathrm{ann}}$ with a fixed depreciation rate. This provides an investment path $\{C_t\}_t$ that grows over time as the adaptation stock accumulates.
> 
> For each draw $m$, temperature level $\Delta T_t$, and investment $C_t$, we then compute the adapted excess deaths
> \[
> D_m^{\mathrm{adapt}}(\Delta T_t, C_t) =
> \begin{cases}
> D_m^{\mathrm{base}}(\Delta T_t)\,\bigl[1 - B_m(C_t,\Delta T_t)\bigr], & \text{if adaptation effectiveness is temperature dependent},\\[0.5em]
> D_m^{\mathrm{base}}(\Delta T_t)\,\exp\bigl(\kappa_m(C_t)\bigr), & \text{if adaptation is represented as a temperature-independent scaling},
> \end{cases}
> \]
> where the first case is used for measures such as shutters (combining temperature and cost responsiveness) and the second for measures such as air conditioning, with $\kappa_m(C_t)$ summarising the cost-based attenuation factor for draw $m$. For each scenario and year, we evaluate the ratio of adapted to baseline deaths, $D_m^{\mathrm{adapt}} / D_m^{\mathrm{base}}$, and summarise this across draws to obtain central estimates and uncertainty bounds for the proportional reduction in mortality under a given annual adaptation budget. These proportional reductions are finally applied to the monetary mortality damages from the baseline risk model (valued using the UK value of a prevented fatality and expressed relative to GDP), yielding temperature- and investment-dependent adaptation-adjusted health damage functions for each scenario.
> ```
