> ```latex
> \subsection{Variability and extreme events}
> 
> To capture both interannual variability in heat-related mortality and the implications of rare extreme events, we construct an ensemble of annual county-level projections and then derive two types of outcomes: expected annual damages (EAD) and 1-in-200-year extreme events. All quantities are expressed as excess heat-related deaths relative to a historical baseline.
> 
> We begin from an ensemble of projected heat-related death rates by county $i$, scenario, and projection period, derived from a climate–health modelling framework (as described in the main health-damages section). Each ensemble member represents one possible realisation of the climate–health pathway. Within each combination of scenario, county, and period, we rank ensemble members by their projected death rate and use this ranking to define a severity index for each realisation. For each scenario–county–realisation triple, we then randomly sample a severity pathway from this ranked set and construct an annual time series of death rates for $t=2025,\dots,2075$ by linear interpolation between anchor years corresponding to approximately 2020, 2050, and 2090. This procedure preserves cross-sectional heterogeneity and the distribution of severity across ensemble members while providing annually resolved trajectories.
> 
> For EAD, we distinguish two climate scenario groupings: a ``Most Likely'' ensemble and a ``High Risk'' ensemble. The ``Most Likely'' category is defined by selecting those climate realisations whose global mean temperature trajectories lie within an externally defined likely range for the relevant emissions pathways, while the ``High Risk'' category corresponds to a high-emissions socio-economic pathway. For each scenario grouping, county $i$, and year $t$ (2025–2075), we compute the mean and empirical 5th and 95th percentiles across ensemble members $m$ of the annual death rate. These statistics are interpreted as the EAD and associated uncertainty range at the county scale. We then aggregate across counties to obtain national totals in each year and scenario.
> 
> Extreme events are analysed only for the ``High Risk'' climate pathway. For each target year $t_0\in\{2030, 2040, 2050\}$, we consider the 10-year window from $t_0$ to $t_0+9$. Within this window, we convert county-level death rates to per-capita terms using county populations and compute, for each year $t$ and county $i$, the ensemble mean and 5th and 95th percentiles of per-capita deaths. To estimate the tail behaviour of county-level outcomes, we apply a pre-calibrated statistical model that maps mean per-capita mortality to an adjusted high-quantile estimate. This model is fitted externally using residual-based methods and, when applied here, is evaluated with stochastic residual draws to propagate model uncertainty and unobserved variability. The resulting adjusted per-capita means and quantiles are then scaled back to counts using county populations.
> 
> To identify the extreme event within each 10-year window, we sum the adjusted 95th-percentile deaths across counties for each year $t$ in the window and select the year with the maximum national total. The corresponding county-level mean and 5th and 95th percentile deaths in that ``worst'' year are recorded as the extreme-event outcome for window $t_0$. For reporting and plotting, the extreme event is associated with the window’s start year $t_0$, indicating that the identified event occurs within the decade beginning in $t_0$.
> 
> The combined dataset thus contains, for each county $i$, year $t$, and scenario category, both EAD (annual mean and uncertainty range) and, for selected decades under the high-risk pathway, the spatial pattern and uncertainty range of extreme heat-related mortality. National-level summaries are obtained by summing county statistics, preserving consistency between local and national results. These outcomes are then used to characterise both typical annual impacts and low-probability, high-impact events within the broader assessment.
> 
